{"sentence": "Disentitlement is a period when a claimant (person proving entitlement) gets no benefits because they failed to prove it, for example by missing information, availability (work readiness), or unemployment proof.", "label": "supported", "rationale": "The context states disentitlements are imposed for failure to do something to prove entitlement (e.g., missing information, availability, unemployment) and that no benefits are paid for days of disentitlement; it also notes disentitlements can last from one day to indefinite, supporting the idea of a period.", "excerpt": "Disentitlements are imposed for something the claimant has failed to do to prove entitlement to benefits, for example, failed to provide information... failed to prove their availability for work, failed to prove they are unemployed.; Disentitlements are imposed for as little as one day, or for an indefinite period of time.; Benefits are not paid, or deemed to be paid, for any days of disentitlement."}
